  "body": {
    "id": "thm:quotient-efficiency",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Suppose that \\(\\mathcal T_P=\\mathcal C_P^\\perp\\).  Suppose also that there are bridges \\(h_a\\in\\mathcal H_a(P)\\) and \\(q_a\\in\\mathcal Q_a(P)\\) for which \\(\\phi_{h,q}\\in L_0^2(P)\\), and that the following pathwise product condition holds.  For every admissible DQM path \\(t\\mapsto P_t\\) with score \\(s\\), there are \\(h_{a,t}\\in\\mathcal H_a(P_t)\\) and \\(q_{a,t}\\in\\mathcal Q_a(P_t)\\) such that, with \\(T_{a,t}\\) and \\(T_{a,t}^*\\) denoting the operators under \\(P_t\\),\n\\[\n \\min\\!\\left\\{\n \\|T_{a,t}(h_{a,t}-h_a)\\|_{\\mathcal G_a(P_t)}\n \\|q_{a,t}-q_a\\|_{\\mathcal G_a(P_t)},\n \\|h_{a,t}-h_a\\|_{\\mathcal H(P_t)}\n \\|T_{a,t}^*(q_{a,t}-q_a)\\|_{\\mathcal H(P_t)}\n \\right\\}=o(|t|)\n\\]\nfor \\(a=0,1\\).  Then \\(\\theta\\) is pathwise differentiable at \\(P\\), and \\(\\phi_{h,q}\\) represents its derivative along every actual path.  Moreover, every gradient is in \\(\\mathcal A_P\\), and, for any \\(\\phi_0\\in\\Phi_P\\),\n\\[\n \\phi_{\\mathrm{eff}}\n =\\phi_0-\\Pi_{\\mathcal C_P}\\phi_0\n =\\operatorname*{argmin}_{\\varphi\\in\\mathcal A_P}E[\\varphi^2],\n \\qquad\n V_{\\mathrm{eff}}(P)=\\inf_{\\varphi\\in\\mathcal A_P}E[\\varphi^2].\n\\]\nNo converse from pathwise differentiability to \\(\\Phi_P\\neq\\varnothing\\) is asserted.",
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:dominated-law",
      "ass:local-uniform-equivalence",
      "def:local-operator-chart",
      "def:joint-model",
      "def:score-family",
      "def:normal-space",
      "def:affine-score-hull",
      "def:quotient-gradient",
      "lem:normal-affine-identity"
    ],
    "justification": "The Hilbert projection and complete gradient-affine-space calculation are proved once tangent equality and bridge-score pathwise validity are available.",
    "gap": "Neither reverse score lifting nor unbounded-product bridge-score validity, including the claimed necessity of \\(\\Phi_P\\ne\\varnothing\\), follows from the frozen assumptions.",
    "consumer": "Analysts must use the unconditional positive finite results or state both missing premises explicitly.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix an admissible path \\(P_t\\) and write \\(r_t=dP_t/dP\\) and \\(z_t=\\sqrt{r_t}-1\\).  By DQM, \\(z_t=ts/2+o_{L^2(P)}(t)\\), and hence \\(\\|z_t\\|_2=O(|t|)\\).  The likelihood-ratio ball in \\(\\mathrm{def:local-operator-chart}\\), together with \\(0<\\varepsilon<1\\), bounds \\(r_t\\) above and away from zero, so \\(\\sup_t\\|z_t\\|_\\infty<\\infty\\) locally.  Therefore every fixed \\(f\\in L^2(P)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n E_{P_t}f-E_Pf\n =E_P\\{f(2z_t+z_t^2)\\}\n =tE_P(fs)+o(t).\n\\]\nIndeed, the linear term follows by Cauchy--Schwarz.  For the quadratic term, for every \\(M>0\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{E_P(|f|z_t^2)}{|t|}\n \\le M\\frac{\\|z_t\\|_2^2}{|t|}\n +\\|f\\mathbf 1\\{|f|>M\\}\\|_2\n   \\frac{\\|z_t\\|_\\infty\\|z_t\\|_2}{|t|}.\n\\]\nThe first term tends to zero for fixed \\(M\\), the second has limsup bounded by a constant times \\(\\|f\\mathbf 1\\{|f|>M\\}\\|_2\\), and this last norm tends to zero as \\(M\\to\\infty\\).  Thus the displayed expectation derivative is justified without assuming bounded bridges.\n\nPut \\(B_{h,q}=\\sum_{a=0}^1t_aS_a(h_a,q_a)=\\theta(P)+\\phi_{h,q}\\).  The base score is square-integrable by hypothesis, so the preceding calculation gives\n\\[\n E_{P_t}B_{h,q}-E_PB_{h,q}\n =tE_P(\\phi_{h,q}s)+o(t),\n\\]\nwhere \\(E_Ps=0\\) removes the centering constant.  It remains to compare \\(E_{P_t}B_{h,q}\\) with \\(\\theta(P_t)\\).  First, the arm mean does not depend on the selected outcome bridge: if \\(h_{a,t}'\\) is another solution, then \\(u=h_{a,t}'-h_{a,t}\\in\\ker T_{a,t}\\), and any \\(q_{a,t}\\in\\mathcal Q_a(P_t)\\) gives\n\\[\n E_{P_t}u=E_{P_t}(I_aq_{a,t}u)\n =E_{P_t}\\{I_aq_{a,t}T_{a,t}u\\}=0.\n\\]\nAll products are integrable by Cauchy--Schwarz and the square-integrability of the two bridges under \\(P_t\\).  Thus \\(\\theta_a(P_t)=E_{P_t}h_{a,t}\\) is well defined.  For one arm, outcome-bridge solvability under \\(P_t\\) gives\n\\[\n E_{P_t}\\!\\left[I_aq_a\\{Y-h_{a,t}(W,X)\\}\\right]=0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(q_a\\) is a function of \\((Z,X)\\) and \\(E_{P_t}[Y-h_{a,t}(W,X)\\mid A=a,Z,X]=0\\).  Treatment-bridge solvability under \\(P_t\\) gives\n\\[\n E_{P_t}\\!\\left[(1-I_aq_{a,t})\\{h_{a,t}(W,X)-h_a(W,X)\\}\\right]=0.\n\\]\nSubtracting these two identities from the definition of \\(S_a\\) yields the exact mixed-bias identity\n\\[\n \\theta_a(P_t)-E_{P_t}S_a(h_a,q_a)\n =E_{P_t}\\!\\left[I_a(q_{a,t}-q_a)(h_{a,t}-h_a)\\right],\n \\qquad \\theta_a(P_t)=E_{P_t}h_{a,t}.\n\\]\nConditioning first on \\((A,Z,X)\\), or first on \\((W,X)\\), and applying Cauchy--Schwarz gives respectively\n\\[\n \\left|\\theta_a(P_t)-E_{P_t}S_a(h_a,q_a)\\right|\n \\le\n \\|T_{a,t}(h_{a,t}-h_a)\\|_{\\mathcal G_a(P_t)}\n \\|q_{a,t}-q_a\\|_{\\mathcal G_a(P_t)}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n \\left|\\theta_a(P_t)-E_{P_t}S_a(h_a,q_a)\\right|\n \\le\n \\|h_{a,t}-h_a\\|_{\\mathcal H(P_t)}\n \\|T_{a,t}^*(q_{a,t}-q_a)\\|_{\\mathcal H(P_t)}.\n\\]\nThe assumed minimum of these products is \\(o(|t|)\\) in each arm.  Taking the signed arm difference proves\n\\[\n \\theta(P_t)-\\theta(P)=tE_P(\\phi_{h,q}s)+o(t).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\phi_{h,q}\\) is a gradient on the actual score set, and continuity extends the representation to \\(\\mathcal T_P\\).\n\nBy \\(\\mathrm{lem:normal-affine-identity}\\), \\(\\mathcal A_P=\\phi_0+\\mathcal C_P\\).  The point\n\\[\n \\phi_* =\\phi_0-\\Pi_{\\mathcal C_P}\\phi_0,\n \\qquad \\phi_*\\perp\\mathcal C_P.\n\\]\nbelongs to \\(\\phi_0+\\mathcal C_P\\).  For every \\(c\\in\\mathcal C_P\\), orthogonality gives\n\\[\n \\|\\phi_0+c\\|_2^2\n =\\|\\phi_*\\|_2^2+\\|\\Pi_{\\mathcal C_P}\\phi_0+c\\|_2^2,\n\\]\nso \\(\\phi_*\\) is the unique minimum-norm point.\nThe assumed tangent equality makes \\(\\phi_*\\in\\mathcal T_P\\).  For \\(v\\in\\mathcal T_P\\),\n\\[\n E(\\phi_*v)=E(\\phi_0v)-E\\{(\\Pi_{\\mathcal C_P}\\phi_0)v\\}=\\dot\\theta_P(v),\n\\]\nso \\(\\phi_*\\) is the canonical gradient.  If \\(g\\in L_0^2(P)\\) is any other gradient, then \\(g-\\phi_0\\in\\mathcal T_P^\\perp=\\mathcal C_P\\); conversely, every \\(\\phi_0+c\\), \\(c\\in\\mathcal C_P\\), represents the same derivative.  Hence the gradient set is exactly \\(\\mathcal A_P\\), and minimizing its squared norm proves both asserted formulas.  None of these steps proves that an arbitrary Riesz representer must be an individual bridge-pair score, which is why no converse is claimed.",
